The touch capability layer: everything gated on the canonical touch gate
(touchGate.ts - hover: none / any-pointer: coarse) that a hover-and-fine-
pointer editor needs to work with a coarse pointer and a soft keyboard. The
narrow-viewport axis is narrow.diff; the Android EditContext vendor-defect
workaround keeps its own patch so the day the defect dies, its patch does too.

Editor. PointerEventHandler is selected on the touch gate, not the phone UA -
upstream's (isIOS || isAndroid+isMobile) leaves Android tablets (no "Mobi" UA
token) and touch laptops on the legacy TouchHandler, which has none of this.
Touch selection is model-driven: the browser's selection UI aims at the
editor's hidden one-pixel input at garbage geometry (device-verified), so
long-press word-selects through the model, the shared selection handles
(touchSelectionHandles.ts, the same grips the terminal uses) adjust it against
a model-coordinate anchor that survives the drag's edge auto-scroll, and a
hold inside a selection opens the IDE context menu. Pans are classified before
the tap path can focus the hidden input; keyboard-driven layout preserves
scroll; mouse presses fall through to the mousedown flow so click counts
survive. What a press turns out to have meant, and where the selection's edges
sit, are overlay modules this calls (touchPress.ts, touchSelection.ts): a tap, a
scroll, a word select and the hold menu are each other's alternatives and the
rules deciding between them are worth running, not reading off a diff.

Menus and lists. Long-press opens context menus wherever right-click does
(editor tabs, composite bars, view panes, status bar, lists), with the native
context menu suppressed while a gesture-opened menu is up; menu placement
respects the visual viewport; tap focuses lists/terminal on Tap, never on
touchstart, so a scroll gesture cannot pop the keyboard; taps consumed by
inline action controls do not also select the row; hover-revealed action bars
stay visible (touch.css pairs each forced bar with the upstream hover rule). A
menu opened by a finger never takes DOM focus: focus is what closes the soft
keyboard, that closing resizes whatever the menu was opened over, and a terminal
answers a row-count change by dropping its selection - so the menu a long-press
selection asked for used to destroy that selection as it opened (device-verified,
and the keybar holds its dock through a menu for the same reason). Items are
tapped, not walked with arrow keys, and Escape still cancels.

Soft keyboard. Whether one is up is answered in exactly one place
(softKeyboard.ts) because browsers expose two shapes: one shrinks the visual
viewport, while one shrinks both viewports in lockstep and is visible only
through shell.js's published verdict. Everything that has to know goes through
it - the workbench fit, the keybar, the editor's tap-to-refocus - so no surface
can measure a number that is structurally zero on its own platform. layout()
fits the workbench to the visual viewport and the edge-to-edge bottom inset -
gated on the touch capability, not a vendor list: where no keyboard
exists it reads 0 (Android device-verified; other coarse-pointer platforms
reasoned, not device-tested). The Keybar docks the keys a soft keyboard cannot
produce (Esc/Tab/arrows/F-keys/sticky modifiers) above it, reserved out of the
grid, while a keyboard is up or a terminal holds focus and never otherwise.
IME line-break commits replay as cancelable Enter keydowns (imeEnter.ts)
carrying the keybar's sticky modifiers; single-line inputs ask the IME for a
newline key (enterkeyhint=enter - an action key renders as Gboard's arrow and
emits nothing); quick input prompts show OK on touch as the IME-independent
accept path. Nothing forces the keyboard down: it follows focus, and a pan
neither pops nor closes it. Transient overlays (quick input, context menus)
dismiss on a deliberate press outside them, never on focus-out - on touch the
soft keyboard churns focus with no intent to dismiss (opening a menu blurs the
field, the keyboard closing, iOS pulling focus to <body>), so a blur is not a
dismissal and ignoreFocusOut is honoured. For a context menu that press has
nowhere to land but the full-screen block the context view puts up, which is a
child of the container the outside-press listener treats as inside - so the
block itself carries the dismissal, or the menu has none at all but Escape
(device-verified: the press blurred the surface underneath and left an invisible
block over a workbench that answered nothing). Nothing autofocuses a search field on
touch (settings, keybindings, extensions, search, SCM - upstream exempted iOS
only); editables keep the OS text-selection menu instead of the void - all but the
invisible IME shims the editor and the terminal park inside their own text, which
touch.css takes out of hit-testing so a hold there reaches the surface it looks
like it landed on (device-verified: it reached nothing at all before).

Terminal. Focus on Tap, and the soft keyboard on a tap and nothing else
(touchKeyboard.ts). Focus is not the lever here that it is everywhere else:
xterm holds its hidden textarea focused for as long as the terminal is the
active surface, so the caret stays visible with the keyboard dismissed and
focus() raises nothing. What raises it again is the browser, for any press
landing on the focused editable - which on a phone is every swipe of the
buffer - and it does so even though this layer cancels the whole sequence
(device-verified: touchstart, every touchmove and touchend all report
defaultPrevented, no Tap is emitted, no focus lever is called, and the keyboard
still comes up). preventDefault does not reach that decision, so the hold is
taken away from the browser with inputmode="none" between taps.
autocomplete=off so Android IMEs drop the suggestion
strip over shell input; the initial hint is tappable (Gesture.ignoreTarget).
Text selection is a whole contribution of ours in the overlay
(terminal.touchSelection.contribution.ts) - a hold word-selects through xterm's
own selection service, the shared handles adjust the range, and a hold on the
selection lets the pane's menu open over it - so all this patch owes it is the
contribution import, plus the selection service on the xterm-private surface and
a selection-change refresh of the selection context keys, without which Copy sat
disabled in the very menu the selection was made for (every other refresh hangs
off a mouse or key event, and a hold produces neither).
The keyboard resizes the grid like any other pane change - holding the scroll
position across that is xterm-resize-scroll.diff, which is not touch-gated.

Links. A tap activates what a click activates: detected terminal links
(url/localFile/localFolder - never 'word') hit-tested through the shared cell
geometry, editor links via a cursor-on-link context menu entry, rendered-markdown
links through Gesture Tap.

Chrome. Sash hit area widens on every coarse-pointer device (upstream: iOS
only); selects use the native picker on touch; the footer collapses to give
short layouts its row back.

